WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.180
Alright then, now we're going to temper the chocolate

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.480
which is a necessary process to be able to work with the chocolate

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.740
Always, whether we want to make an artistic piece, chocolates or a chocolate bar.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.780
We always have to temper the chocolate.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.060
Tempering the chocolate means

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.400
to force crystals to crystallize in the correct form

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.240
so that afterwards, the texture sheen and snap of the chocolate will be correct.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.200
The traditional way, nowadays the tempering is done with machines,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.080
but the homemade method that we still use in our workshops

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.940
the way to temper chocolate is to cool it down on a cold surface.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.640
So this is a cold marble slab.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:53.680
We cast the chocolate that is at 45-50C on the marble but we leave a small fraction in the bowl.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.080
That small fraction will allow us later to do the second part of the tempering process.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.000
Therefore, that chocolate, what we do is that we spread it

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.120
for it to cool down homogeneously.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.260
Therefore when that chocolate reaches about 28C

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:24.920
we mix it back with the rest which is at 45C to achieve a final mixture of 30-31C.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.100
Depending upon the type of chocolate,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.860
not all types of chocolates need to be tempered at the same temperature.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.840
For dark chocolate we'd be working at the temperatures I mentioned

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.580
while for milk chocolate or white chocolate we'd have to work at slightly lower temperatures.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.300
So once we obtain this, what we do is that we mix

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.420
homogeneously

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.040
the two temperatures.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.840
And theoretically, if I've done it right the final result should be a chocolate at 30-31C

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:08.240
which would be the optimal way to obtain a chocolate with the fifth crystalline form.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.460
So the first thing we can do when we have tempered chocolate is to make a chocolate bar.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.060
If we have a mold, we cast it inside the mold

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:21.600
we vibrate it

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.780
and now we would let it cool down, and after a few minutes

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.600
we'd have a chocolate that is tempered

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.440
and would have a good snap and sheen.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.600
PROFESSOR: So you've just seen
remarkable chocolate creations

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.420
from Enric Rovira And
now what we want to do

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.990
is take a little bit of time and talk
to you about the physics of chocolate.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.615
Now as you will see, the discussion
of the physics of chocolate

00:02:45.615 --> 00:02:49.770
is similar to the discussion
of the physics of fats

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:54.360
that we had a little bit earlier in this
course, because chocolate, of course,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.870
is made primarily out of cocoa
butter, which is itself a fat.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.440
You will recall that
cocoa butter, that fats,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.040
that is the fats that are in food, tend
to be in the form of triglycerides.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.410
They have molecular
structures that are like this,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.950
so they sort of have two long arms
that are stuck to a third arm which

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.570
is coming off on the end.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.810
And it's the interactions
between such molecules

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:19.280
that actually give rise to
the phase behavior of fats.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.680
Cocoa butter, it turns out, which is
the primary constituent of chocolate,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.327
is a very special kind of fat, and it
has really very special properties.

00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:28.410
And so what we want to do
right now is talk to you

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.480
a little bit about what
those properties are

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.620
and to try to make it so that you
love the properties as much as you

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.060
love the chocolate.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.360
So, a good way to start out and
explain to you how unusual chocolate is

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.270
as a material, is just to
take a piece of chocolate

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.270
and to think about it a little
bit and about the properties

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.760
that you all know and love
when you eat chocolate.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.120
And you saw this, of course, very
much, when you watched Enric.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.390
So chocolate has a couple of properties
that I would like to highlight.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.980
One is that it is, of course, a solid.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.370
It is a solid it does
not melt in your hand.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.570
The fact that it's a solid-- you
can tell, because if you break it,

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.940
it sort of snaps.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.119
There's a snap of chocolate that occurs
when you take a nice piece of chocolate

00:04:09.119 --> 00:04:09.660
and break it.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.339
And that's sort of a typical
characteristic of a solid.

00:04:12.339 --> 00:04:14.880
The other thing about chocolate
is if you take a piece of it,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.450
as I now will, and put it in your mouth,
sorry, then it melts in your mouth

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.800
as you're eating it.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.480
And so, if you think about it, it's
really a very unusual situation.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.724
Chocolate does not melt in your
hands, but it does melt in your mouth.

00:04:30.724 --> 00:04:33.390
You know, it's something like a
10 degree temperature difference

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.900
between your mouth and your hands, but
chocolate doesn't melt in your hands

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.970
but it does melt in your
mouth, and that's really

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.440
one of its essential properties.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.880
If we go back to our discussion before
of fats and their melting behavior,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.350
then you will recall that different
fats have different melting points.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.100
And the differences in the melting
points, we explained before,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.320
by the molecular structure of the fats.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.140
We basically pointed out that fats
could be saturated or unsaturated.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.850
That saturated fats, you will
remember, pack together very well.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.890
The molecules stick to
each other very strongly,

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.910
whereas unsaturated fats were harder
to pack and didn't stick as strongly,

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.100
and therefore saturated
fats had higher melting

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.226
temperatures than unsaturated fats.

00:05:13.226 --> 00:05:15.600
Now if you look at the molecular
structure of chocolate--

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.780
as I said before, it's
a triglyceride-- there

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.880
are sort of three different
acids that make up

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.030
the three different arms of chocolate.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:26.910
There's palmitic acid and stearic
acid, which are on the two side arms,

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.370
and on the long arm is a oleic acid.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.570
The side armed acids are both
saturated, oleic acid is unsaturated,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.380
and it's the packing behavior of such
molecules as a function of temperature

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.170
that basically gives rise
to the physics of chocolate,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.490
which I now want to discuss.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:45.731
Many fats actually have only one
solid phase, but it turns out--

00:05:45.731 --> 00:05:48.230
and I'm going to try to explain
to you what I mean by this--

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.150
that cocoa butter, that is the
fat that underlies chocolate,

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.330
has actually six different solid phases.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.670
So this is the phase
diagram of chocolate.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.730
So chocolate has, as I
said, six crystalline phases

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:03.390
that vary in temperature from
18 degrees to 36 degrees.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:07.260
So the lowest phase, which
people call phase one, cleverly,

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.630
has a melting temperature
of about 18 degrees.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:13.380
The second phase, which people call
phase two, is about 22 degrees.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.790
And then there are other phases.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:18.480
There's phase four, which is 34 degrees,
and phase six which is 36 degrees,

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:19.450
and so on and so forth.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:21.180
And these are the six different phases.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.920
And in fact, it turns out that five
and six are the are the ones that--

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.320
and in particular, phase five--

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.660
is the phase that you
need to hit in order

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:34.260
to make the chocolate that you know and
love and that we saw so spectacularly

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.840
illustrated by Enric.

